  "_note": "RUN 4 of the series -- sub-4s dwell, the delivery knee and the throughput optimum. 5 levels d1000/d2000/d3000/d4000/d6000 = 1/2/3/4/6s per mid, 30 cells/level interleaved (5-cell blocks cycle all levels), 20 windows/cell. Iso-broadcast on the series' standard transmit axis (adv scales with dwell, 5 broadcasts/key), so it extends the dwell axis of run 2 downward on the same footing rather than measuring dwell confounded with broadcast count. ~3.2h transmit. RUN THIS LAST and at the attenuation run 2 shows to be off the ceiling: at full power the dwell factorial already delivered 100% at 4s, so a full-power pass here is expected to stay flat and answer nothing -- the knee only exists below the ceiling. Together runs 2 and 4 give dwell from 1s to 8s at one power setting. NOTE the key rate at d1000 is ~60/min, well past even the standard 256 soft cap, so propagation coverage will be lower at the short-dwell levels; deliverability is unaffected (offline resweep). Ground truth: resweep.py. ANCHOR CELLS: cells named a###_ref are the series' shared reference condition (incremental, adv 1s, 8s dwell, 20 windows, +9 dBm) -- the same cell the density matrix repeats 120 times. One opens every run and one follows every few blocks, ~10% of the run. They are not part of this matrix's design; they exist so any two nights can be compared against a condition they actually share, and so a night whose ambient density was unusual announces itself instead of silently biasing the comparison. Analyse them across runs first, then read the run's own contrast. ANTENNA STATE: directional antenna FITTED -- the series BASELINE state. Runs 1, 3 and 4 all use it, so their anchor cells form one comparable chain; only the txpower_dwell_noant half departs from it. If you run this with the antenna off, its anchors do NOT compare against the rest of the series -- record it and say so.",
